===Verb===
{{en-verb|~s|~ning|overran|~}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[defeat]] an [[enemy]] and [[invade]] in great [[number]]s, [[seize|seizing]] the enemy [[position]]s [[conclusively]].
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|date=26 Apr 2000|author=Walter A. McDougall|title=Who Were We in Vietnam?|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2000/04/26/opinion/who-were-we-in-vietnam.html|accessdate=3 Aug. 2008
|passage=But news that the North Vietnamese had launched an invasion more akin to the blitzkrieg of Poland than to a Maoist "people's war," and had '''overrun''' my old base camp 30 miles up Thunder Road from Saigon, turned my heart to lead.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20150527141009/https://www.nytimes.com/2000/04/26/opinion/who-were-we-in-vietnam.html}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[infest]], [[swarm]] over, [[flow]] over.
#: {{cot|en|run over#Verb<qq:intransitive>}}
#: {{ux|en|The cathedral square was overrun with tourists.}}
#: {{ux|en|The vine '''overran''' its trellis; the field is '''overrun''' with weeds.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Ireland|passage=those barbarous nations that '''over-ran''' the world}}
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|date=November 7, 2012|author=Matt Bai|title=Winning a Second Term, Obama Will Confront Familiar Headwinds|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/07/us/politics/in-president-obamas-second-term-familiar-challenges.html| passage=As President Obama turns his attention once again to filling out a cabinet and writing an Inaugural Address, this much is clear: he should not expect to bask in a surge of national unity, or to witness a crowd of millions '''overrun''' the Mall just to say they were there.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210507013624/https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/07/us/politics/in-president-obamas-second-term-familiar-challenges.html}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[run]] [[past]]; to run beyond.
#: {{cot|en|run over#Verb<qq:intransitive>}}
#: {{ux|en|The athlete '''overran''' the finish line and kept going.}}
#: {{ux|en|One line '''overruns''' another in length.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|2 Samuel|18|23|passage=Ahimaaz ran by the way of the plain, and '''overran''' Cushi.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To run past the [[end]] of.
##: {{cot|en|run over#Verb<qq:intransitive>}}
##: {{ux|en|The aircraft didn't stop in time and '''overran''' the runway.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[continue]] for too long.
#: {{syn|en|run over#Verb<qq:intransitive>}}
#: {{ux|en|The performance '''overran''' by ten minutes, which caused some people to miss their bus home.}}
# {{lb|en|printing|transitive}} To [[carry]] (some [[type]], a [[line]] or [[column]], etc.) [[backward]] or [[forward]] into an [[adjacent]] line or [[page]].
#: {{syn|en|run over#Verb<qq:transitive>}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[go beyond]]; to [[extend]] in part beyond.
#: {{ux|en|In machinery, a sliding piece is said to '''overrun''' its bearing when its forward end goes beyond it.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=9 Jun 24|author={{w|maia arson crimew}}|work=maia blog|url=https://maia.crimew.gay/posts/fuckstalkerware-7/|title=israeli national police found trying to purchase stalkerware|text=all of these services, including mSpy, function with the same kind of predatory subscription scheme, luring you in with free trials that autorenew as abhorrently expensive monthly or yearly subscriptions. canceling the service requires reaching out to support, so users are typically unable to avoid '''overrunning''' the trial.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240823004621/https://maia.crimew.gay/posts/fuckstalkerware-7/}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[abuse]] or [[oppress]], as if by [[tread]]ing upon.
#: {{syn|en|run over#Verb<qq:transitive>}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=V|canto=II|stanza=9|passage=None of them the feeble '''overren'''.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=2005
|author=w:Jesse Helms
|chapter=Jimmy Carter
|title=Here's Where I Stand: A Memoir
|url=https://archive.org/details/hereswhereistand0000helm/
|location=New York
|publisher=w:Random House
|ISBN=0-375-50884-8
|LCCN=2005042795
|OCLC=835465798
|pages=106–107
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/hereswhereistand0000helm/page/228/
|text=Fortunately, in April 1979, Congress codified the United States' support for the brave people of the Republic of China with the passage of the Taiwan Relations Act. This gave our friends the cover to develop the vibrant economy and flourishing democracy we see today and prevented them from being '''overrun''' by the same tyrants who are now in the process of eradicating democracy in Hong Kong.}}

