===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# An [[instance]] of [[#Verb|overrun]]ning.
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|date=2013 June 18|author=w:Simon Romero|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/19/world/americas/brazilian-leaders-brace-for-more-protests.html|title=Protests Widen as Brazilians Chide Leaders|text=Some of the stadiums being built for the World Cup soccer tournament, scheduled for next year, have also been criticized for delays and cost '''overruns''', and have become subjects of derision as protesters question whether they will become white elephants.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20200811161418/https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/19/world/americas/brazilian-leaders-brace-for-more-protests.html}}
#* {{RQ:Time|date=2021-12-13|author=Molly Ball; Jeffrey Kluger; Alejandro de la Garza|title=Elon Musk: Person of the Year 2021|url=https://time.com/person-of-the-year-2021-elon-musk/|text=Tesla’s first decade was plagued by unmet deadlines, technical snafus and cost '''overruns'''.|accessdate=2021-12-31|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20211213124147/https://time.com/person-of-the-year-2021-elon-musk/|archivedate=13 December 2021}}
## {{lb|en|printing}} A [[turnover#Noun|turnover]]: a break to a new line by text flowing within the column.
##: {{syn|en|runover#Noun}}
# The amount by which something [[#Verb|overrun]]s.
#: {{ux|en|At least this year's '''overrun''' isn't as unmanageable as last year!}}
# {{lb|en|aviation}} An area of [[terrain]] beyond the end of a [[runway]] that is kept [[flat]] and [[unobstructed]] to allow an [[aircraft]] that runs off the end of the runway to [[stop]] safely.
#: {{syn|en|runway safety area|stopway}}
#: {{ux|en|The runway has 7,000 feet of paved surface, plus a 2,000-foot grass '''overrun'''.}}
# {{lb|en|food}} [[air|Air]] that is [[whip]]ped into a [[frozen]] [[dessert]] to make it easier to serve and eat.

